Photometric lighting and block-light calibration
================================================

Source proposal: docs/PBR.PhotometricLighting.Proposal.md
Status: Phases 1-5 complete after second review and independent completion audit (2026-10-12).
Shared shader conversion, storage support, physical producer calibration and receiver normalization are implemented.
Only Phase 1-5 completion markers are checked; integrated qualification remains open.
The approved defensive storage helper, scene and postprocessing write boundaries are complete; integrated qualification remains open in Phase 6. It does not
retroactively claim that completed Phase 2 support already implements those safeguards.
The proposal remains authoritative. Unresolved design choices are prerequisite tasks, not
settled defaults or authorization for speculative systems.

Goal and scope
  Express source output, incident illumination and outgoing brightness in compatible photometric
  quantities before display conversion. Calibrate the existing engine block-light field entirely
  in shared VGE shader code. Reuse existing lighting, atmosphere, LumOn, binding, resource and
  exposure owners; preserve standalone PBR and both lighting modes.
  This is an RGB approximation with physical units. Keep engine light generation, propagation,
  attenuation, enclosure behavior and light data unchanged. Existing VGE shader injection may
  change how those inputs are interpreted; do not add CPU lighting/propagation patches.
  Exclude spectral rendering, new voxel propagation, source discovery, source-based replacement
  of block lighting, general area-light transport and unrelated attachment redesign. Photographic
  EV100 authoring was excluded from Phases 1-6; the user-requested Phase 7 adds the bounded
  exposure migration below. Use the approved pre-exposed frame lighting, bounded visible solar disk and fixed-scale
  persistent lighting contract in the proposal. The approved solar-only finite-angular specular
  refinement is the narrow exception to general area-light transport. Do not strengthen AO to hide lighting errors.
  The approved bounded water-source refinement is the narrow exception to retaining the existing
  heterogeneous scattering estimator; preserve medium coefficients, transmission and capture ownership.
  Do not release an intermediate mix of legacy and physical lighting quantities.

Design decisions and remaining prerequisites
  - Retain one metre per world block, verified at existing atmosphere/water/coordinate boundaries;
    keep atmosphere kilometres explicit at shared spatial boundaries.
  - Retain 100,000 lux at perpendicular incidence with zenith sun, altitude 0.001 km and aerosol
    setting 1, preserving existing solar chromaticity. This is a chosen VGE calibration condition,
    not a universal real-world measurement; downstream CPU/GPU qualification remains required.
  - Use the normal torch and documented nominal 44 lm isotropic equivalent as the reference
    candidate. Fit reference block-field lux, engine-point-light mapping and emissive reference
    nits from source-derived engine-field and compatibility evidence.
    Current engine light levels do not establish individual lamp output in lumens.
  - Remove the source-verified neutral floor with the proposal's chroma-preserving shader toe,
    then apply amplitude*q^3/(q^3+0.064), q=amplitude, and referenceLux=12.8574078602199 for the
    nominated active-table torch fixture. This approved refinement supersedes the original
    linear amplitude mapping and its 8.982904808459303 multiplier. Retain engine propagation;
    the shader response remains a compatibility mapping, not inverse-square transport.
  - Use one frozen frame pre-exposure, a 64,000 stored-unit visible-disk cap and a fixed persistent
    scale 1/16 within the qualified admission domain. Retain current texture formats except
    the approved RGBA32F storage for the three existing OIT accumulation layers.
    The 0.001-nit/1% stress interval is not an accepted precision requirement.
  - Follow the reconciled LumOn boundary inventory before modifying its representation.
  - The user approved finite-angular solar surface specular response using the existing visible
    disk radius. Select and qualify its concrete approximation and bound before storage acceptance;
    preserve punctual lights, diffuse irradiance, visibility and existing ownership.
  - Apply the recorded analytical/quantization tolerances; establish approximate-transport
    integration/variance receipts before qualification.
    In-game appearance is user-run evidence; do not launch the game for visual acceptance.

Phase 1: Resolve calibration, boundary contracts and storage prerequisites
------------------------------------------------------------------------
Outcome: A measurable design and an accepted storage representation permit coherent conversion
of sources and consumers without guessing intensity constants or buffer meanings.

Completion boundary
  This phase settles the conversion equations, reference mappings, supported storage domains,
  publication/failure rules and numerical qualification of the design. It does not require the
  later pipeline migration to exist. Integrated GPU arithmetic, resource transitions and full
  scene/appearance acceptance remain verification gates of the implementation phases below.
  Their absence is not itself a prerequisite design failure. An unspecified source bound,
  unselected range safeguard or a failed design fixture is still a prerequisite failure;
  do not defer those decisions or relabel conditional receipts as complete qualification.

Current prerequisite evidence (2026-10-12)
  - The proposal's "Prerequisite source reconciliation" records the source/consumer and storage
    inventories, spatial/solar decisions, fixture protocol and separate quantitative tolerances.
  - User selected the normal torch and requested a research-backed wood/fat reference. The
    inspected experiment supports a nominal 44 lm isotropic equivalent, about 3.5 lux at one
    metre; this is not measured total flux or an observed engine-field calibration.
  - Fresh source-linked numerical and installed-engine IL receipts are under
    artifacts/PhotometricLightingPrerequisites. The illustrative 0.001-nit-to-solar-disk envelope
    has no fixed scale satisfying that illustrative dark precision bound in current scene formats;
    this is not a gate for the subsequently approved bounded pre-exposed representation.
  - Current source and document review plus independent numerical recheck (148 assertions)
    support the investigation. User approval selects the proposal's pre-exposure/disk-cap/cache
    design; a live torch capture is no longer mandatory when source evidence establishes the field.
    Fresh installed-source fixtures and numerical receipts now establish block/point/emission
    compatibility choices. Storage diagnostics passed 806,905 bounded-storage, 2,103 calibration/
    safety and 313 postprocessing assertions; these are CPU/source receipts, not integrated proof.
    Qualification remains unresolved for half-float transparent overdraw and transient range/
    precision. The tighter joint SH bound passes seven numerical assertions; 630 format/display/
    visibility and 45 world-fallback assertions distinguish arithmetic from lighting acceptance.
    The candidate global worst-case pre-exposure guard is rejected: in daylight it rounds the
    calibrated torch on an 18% receiver to zero in packed scene storage. Do not implement it.
    A 120-case preliminary transparent accumulation receipt confirms overflow at eight full-
    coverage layers with stored RGB 16,000; FP32 buckets survive the tested 1,024-layer envelope.
    The user approved the narrow three-bucket RGBA32F exception on 2026-10-11. The allocation hook
    changes the original engine array allocation. Two focused installed/GPU tests pass for
    exact operand selection, actual three-layer storage, resize/clear/sample/retirement and finite
    normalized eight-layer HDR accumulation. Receipts: artifacts/PhotometricLightingPrerequisites/
    OitFormatTests. Full production appearance and live bandwidth/frame costs remain unmeasured.
    The algorithm replacement is a separate future review in docs/PBR.Transparency.Proposal.md,
    tracked by PBR.BaselineShading.todo rather than this photometric implementation plan.
    The 77 conditional numerical checks inform the approved finite-angular solar refinement:
    an ideal passive-reflection bound preserves the reference torch. Approval reconciles
    the scope exclusion; the selected response still needs its own range/precision qualification,
    since an ideal passive-reflection proof does not establish it. The user approved implementing
    representative-direction evaluation with view-dependent energy compensation. The shared
    pbr_finite_disk_brdf helper now supplies deferred, forward and separate liquid surface solar
    reflection, retaining center-direction diffuse/masking and punctual response. Its focused
    validation and photometric storage qualification are separate; the ideal bound is not assumed.
    The selected four-iteration source passes 182 CPU arithmetic/domain checks, with maximum
    half-vector alignment gap 2.89e-8 in 24 independent boundary comparisons. Its conservative
    initial specular bound is six times maximum disk RGB, not an assumed passive bound;
    the subsequent geometry-coupled certificate below supersedes this loose bound.
    Receipts: artifacts/PhotometricLightingPrerequisites/representative-solar-validation.json.
    Recorded exact-disk comparison differences remain approximation limits; full scene storage
    qualification and in-game appearance are not established by these CPU checks.
    Focused installed/GPU validation passes 142 tests with no failures or skips, covering
    directional limits, emitter-radius agreement, independent cone geometry, minimum-roughness
    peak precision, grazing cases and existing deferred/forward/shadow/liquid contracts.
    Production Release build passes. These receipts do not establish live appearance or GPU cost.
    Explicit solar/punctual call routing also passes the same 142 tests and Release build;
    angular radius no longer selects the light type. Receipt:
    artifacts/PhotometricLightingPrerequisites/solar-routing-validation.json.
    Generic finite-disk helper/file names pass the same 142 tests and Release build without
    changing the equations. Receipt:
    artifacts/PhotometricLightingPrerequisites/finite-disk-renaming-validation.json.
    Independent centered normal-view integration reproduces the roughness-0.1 deficit of
    42.17%, primarily from the mixed roughness/source-extent compensation term. Masking,
    dielectric Fresnel and irradiance normalization do not explain the difference. No runtime
    equation changed; term-removal diagnostics do not qualify an off-normal replacement.
    Receipt: artifacts/PhotometricLightingPrerequisites/finite-disk-discrepancy-results.json.
    Geometry-coupled real-arithmetic certificates now bound opaque finite-disk response at
    0.9 times maximum disk RGB and coverage-normalized water reflection at twice disk RGB.
    Punctual bounds are 66,560 times incident RGB for opaque surfaces and 200,000 for
    coverage-normalized water. No runtime equation changed. Receipts:
    artifacts/PhotometricLightingPrerequisites/finite-disk-range-coupling-results.json and
    artifacts/PhotometricLightingPrerequisites/water-punctual-range-coupling-results.json.
    Conditional assembled frame experiments pass 45,238 CPU arithmetic assertions across
    both modes, 0–100 maximum installed-table points and GI gains 1 and 16. All reference
    torch RGB channels survive; worst daylight packed luminance error is 7.35% standalone
    and 7.10% with LumOn, versus 0.56% and 0.34% in half-float. Single-store format tolerances
    pass, but accumulated lighting/color precision and appearance are not established.
    Atmosphere/environment admission, GI tint, transport episode limits, custom point inputs,
    history provenance and GPU arithmetic were unresolved by this original experiment. The
    subsequent selection-rule receipt below does not itself qualify all producer admissions.
    Receipt: artifacts/PhotometricLightingPrerequisites/complete-frame-coupled-water-supplement.json.
    Owner reconciliation finds the homogeneous water budget does not cover signed boundary
    capture with different entry/exit illumination. A valid one-metre interval before a
    100-metre receiver produces blue scattering 62.3394 from endpoint sources bounded by 1;
    the homogeneous control is 0.62969. This is an arithmetic witness, not live appearance.
    Qualification must bound signed source accumulation separately from optical depth/length.
    Receipt: artifacts/PhotometricLightingPrerequisites/water-signed-boundary-range-results.json.
    A narrow signed-water frame supplement passes 99 arithmetic/source-identity assertions.
    At a 1,000-metre daylight receiver, its conditional budget rounds the reference torch's
    packed blue to zero in both modes, with about 55.8% luminance error; half-float retains RGB
    but has about 1.1% error. This is not observed gameplay. Format absolute tolerances pass,
    yet calibrated-lamp precision remains unqualified; do not adopt the tested global guard.
    Receipt: artifacts/PhotometricLightingPrerequisites/complete-frame-signed-water-supplement.json.
    The approved bounded water-source estimator preserves homogeneous behavior and limits
    scattering using admitted source illumination and accepted optical depth. Its proposal-only
    receipt passes 310 CPU assertions; conditional frame fixtures retain all torch channels.
    The user approved this narrow refinement on 2026-10-12; it is not completed runtime work or
    full range/precision acceptance. See proposal "Approved bounded water-source estimator" and
    artifacts/PhotometricLightingPrerequisites/passive-boundary-envelope-proposal-results.json.
    The water-source convention is now explicit: solar lux times phase plus isotropic
    (blockLux+pi*EnvironmentEOverPi)/(4*pi); the camera route has no direct-solar term.
    A 273-check source receipt qualifies U=64*Solar+(Bmax+pi*EnvironmentEOverPi)/(4*pi)
    under the recorded FP32 arithmetic model. Source-expression rounding, publication and
    capture-product validity remain implementation checks; assembled producer admission is open.
    Receipt: artifacts/PhotometricLightingPrerequisites/water-source-upper-qualification.json.
    Select the revised source-informed frame scale rule within its documented arithmetic
    domain. The 20-check receipt covers outward metadata/downward scale rounding, calibrated
    torch RGB retention, per-component originating history bounds and actual artistic/source
    gains. It does not close admission for all producers; do not reuse fixed experimental
    ceilings as actual source bounds. See proposal "Selected scale rule and supported arithmetic
    domain" and artifacts/PhotometricLightingPrerequisites/frame-scale-domain-qualification.json.
    Five source/real-arithmetic OIT checks cover valid negative depths and weight<1.011;
    FP32 accumulation has ample range in the recorded count domain. Rounded logarithm inputs
    and GPU blend precision remain implementation checks, not proof from this range receipt:
    artifacts/PhotometricLightingPrerequisites/oit-real-range-qualification.json.
    Fixed C=1/16 is selected within explicit scalar and directional admission domains.
    Ten checks verify finite endpoints, overflow rejection, signed SH coefficient allowances,
    current certificate sources and convex histories. The proposal's producer-boundary table
    assigns validation/failure ownership; it does not claim legacy runtime enforcement.
    Receipt: artifacts/PhotometricLightingPrerequisites/persistent-admission-qualification.json.
    The explicit assembled B equation now passes nine arithmetic checks across standalone/LumOn
    day/night fixtures. All retain nominated torch RGB and store below 16,000. Actual legacy
    additive bounds are required; the fixture's 32-nit allowance is not a universal effect bound.
    See proposal "Selected scale rule and supported arithmetic domain" and
    artifacts/PhotometricLightingPrerequisites/assembled-frame-budget-qualification.json.
    Fresh held-source inspection covers five installed assemblies and six narrow checks.
    Standard player-held sources publish dynamic arrays; the propagated field reads stored
    chunk lighting seeded by placed blocks. No standard held-to-field feed was found in the
    inspected owner chains. Custom registered/indirect paths remain potentially overlapping;
    no new accounting policy is selected. Receipt:
    artifacts/PhotometricLightingPrerequisites/EngineFieldReceipt/held-source-routing-receipt.json.
    Atmosphere owner/source extraction passes 43 assertions over seven existing complete
    quality-zero fixtures. Packed components fit candidate C=1/16; independent smooth/Mie
    maxima cannot certify the experimental directional ceiling over all accepted inputs.
    Existing completed arrays and packing/upload owners can publish matching-generation bounds
    without a new GPU pass/readback. Fixed-scale range, reconstructed phase/interpolation,
    stored rounding and retained-history bounds still require qualification.
    Receipt: artifacts/PhotometricLightingPrerequisites/atmosphere-publication-range-qualification.json.
    Independent angular references cover nine constant/linear/quadratic cases at three normals.
    Maximum SH9 approximation error is 1.42%; legacy gather errors reach 5.46% at stride one
    and 6.50% at stride two. Controlled 64-ray sampling with history cap four compares measured
    mean/variance with its analytical model; neither diagnostic establishes runtime transport error.
    These receipts preserve existing tolerances and the separate 1.28 joint SH range certificate:
    artifacts/PhotometricLightingPrerequisites/angular-reference-results.json and
    artifacts/PhotometricLightingPrerequisites/angular-variance-results.json.
    A solar-only widened-GGX candidate passes 89 bounded arithmetic checks, with a certified
    2.1-times-disk specular bound. It is not selected: converged finite-disk comparisons show
    substantial off-normal differences, and minimum-roughness references have not converged.
    Storage survival alone does not qualify its material response.
    The final additive inventory resolves the prerequisite gap identified by second review:
    concrete unit adapters and source-expression bounds replace the experimental R=32 allowance.
    Earlier unresolved/rejected receipts above remain historical diagnostics; the selected
    supported domain, producer admissions and current proposal govern implementation.
    The proposal's "Additive compatibility contributors" now selects the existing 32-nit neutral
    reference for authored scene approximations and separates physical producers. Installed
    clouds/particles/celestial/aurora and transparent-compose sources establish explicit expression,
    integration and alpha-normalization boundaries. Volumetric clouds integrate at most 200 samples
    and bypass ordinary OIT premultiplication; the resolve returns black below weighted alpha
    0.0001 and divides otherwise. Nine targeted arithmetic checks pass for traversal, resolve and
    the selected HDR display-noise bypass. The original transform can push alpha above one;
    PBR.OutputDithering.md already requires final-output dithering instead. Actual input bounds
    are now explicit in the proposal. Receipt:
    artifacts/PhotometricLightingPrerequisites/additive-cloud-bound-qualification.json.
    Fifteen final source-linked checks cover those formulas, corrected moon fog ordering,
    night-sky alpha 1.1 and invalid legacy point divisions. The combined conditional cloud/star/
    frame fixture fits the selected retention domain. Receipt:
    artifacts/PhotometricLightingPrerequisites/additive-input-formulas-qualification.json.
    Second review reconciled these findings into the proposal and downstream scene tasks.
    Independent completion audit checked the full task/document mapping against the governing
    proposal and linked contracts: design prerequisites are satisfied within the explicit admitted
    domain. Actual metadata enforcement, integrated GPU conversion/precision, resource transition
    execution and live appearance remain downstream verification, as the completion boundary states.

